  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=f(x-1)+1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the right and get the graph of \n$y=f(x-1).$  After this transformation, the point $(1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1+1,4)=(2,4).\n$$\n\n- Finally,  we shift the result vertically by $1$  unit up and obtain the graph of\n$y=f(x-1)+1.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,4+1)=(2,5).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(1,4)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(1)=4.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=f(x-1)+1.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =f(2-1)+1 \\\\  & =f(1)+1 \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,5)$ must lie on $y=f(x-1)+1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
